package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneRunning is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/Scenes/running.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Learn how Quorum draws graphics with OpenGL, WebGL and Vulkan, load and run a scene, and test physics hypotheses.")

        Title("Running a Scene")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("A scene made in Quorum Studio is a picture of a game's world: where things are,
what they look like and what they are called. It doesn't do anything yet. This page starts with how Quorum
draws graphics at all, through one of three graphics systems: OpenGL, WebGL and Vulkan. It then shows how a
small program loads a scene and starts it in a game window, and what more that program needs for 3D physics
and terrain. Finally, you'll test the hypotheses about physics that you wrote in the last tutorial.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Describe what a graphics engine does, and how OpenGL, WebGL and Vulkan differ.")
        ListItem():Text("Load a scene from a program with "):Code("LoadScene"):Text(", using Vulkan.")
        ListItem("Run a scene from Quorum Studio, and set it up for 3D physics and terrain.")
        ListItem("Test hypotheses about physics, one change at a time, by sight, by sound and by timing, and record the results.")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of a stage set for a play before the actors arrive. The scenery is all in
place, but nothing happens. What has to be added before the play can start? Which parts of a game are like
the scenery, and which are like the actors and the script?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Running/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/scenes/Running/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_DATA_INVESTIGATION_26)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_5)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_13)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHITECTURE_7)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        GraphicsEngines()
        WhatVulkanAdds()
        MakeItReal()
        LoadingProgram()
        RunningTheScene()
        PhysicsAndGravity()
        HearingWhatHappens()
        ScenesWithTerrain()
    end

    private action GraphicsEngines
        Subsection("How Quorum draws: graphics engines")
        Paragraph("Everything you see in a game, from a cube to a mountain to a shadow, has to be drawn on
the screen, many times every second. The part of a game system that does this is called a "):Term("graphics engine"):Text(". It takes everything in the scene, works out what the camera can see, and sends drawing
instructions to the computer's graphics hardware, often called the graphics card or GPU. Quorum's games have a
graphics engine built in, so you don't have to write those instructions yourself.")

        Paragraph("Graphics hardware is made by many companies, so programs talk to it through
shared standards, a bit like how every country's power outlets need plugs that fit. Quorum's engine can use
three of them, under the hood:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("OpenGL"):Text(" came first. It was released in 1992 by Silicon Graphics, a company
that made powerful computers for 3D graphics, and it is now looked after by a group of companies called the
Khronos Group. For more than twenty years, it was the way most programs drew 3D graphics on Windows, Mac and
Linux. Quorum uses OpenGL unless you ask for something else.")
        ListItem():Strong("WebGL"):Text(" brought OpenGL to the web. Released in 2011, also by the Khronos
Group, it lets a web page draw 3D graphics without installing anything. When a Quorum game runs inside a web
browser, it draws with WebGL.")
        ListItem():Strong("Vulkan"):Text(" is the newest. The Khronos Group released it in 2016 as a modern
replacement for OpenGL. It is faster and gives programs much more control over the graphics hardware, which
makes newer effects possible. On a Mac, Vulkan runs on top of Apple's own graphics system through a translator
called MoltenVK.")

        Paragraph("Because Vulkan is the newest, Quorum's newest graphics features need it. To use
everything in the rest of these tutorials, turn Vulkan on in the program that runs your scene, with one line
before "):Code("StartGame()"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/SceneRunning/UseVulkan.quorum")
        Paragraph("If a computer can't run Vulkan, Quorum quietly falls back to OpenGL.")
    end

    private action WhatVulkanAdds
        Subsection("What Vulkan adds in Quorum")
        Paragraph("Turning on Vulkan gives your scenes features that OpenGL in Quorum doesn't have:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Terrain"):Text(": the ground of a 3D world, with hills and valleys you shape in
the scene editor. A scene with a terrain won't load without Vulkan.")
        ListItem():Strong("Shadows"):Text(": lights such as the sun can cast shadows, so objects look like
they're really sitting on the ground.")
        ListItem():Strong("Spotlights"):Text(": cone-shaped lights, like a flashlight or a car's headlights.
Under OpenGL they aren't drawn at all.")
        ListItem():Strong("Realistic materials"):Text(": surfaces made from texture sets, with bumps,
roughness and other details, such as the grass and rock on a terrain.")
        ListItem():Strong("Smoother edges"):Text(": an option called multisampling that softens the jagged "):Quoted("staircase"):Text(" edges on 3D shapes.")
        Paragraph("When Quorum Studio itself runs with Vulkan, its scene editor shows these features
while you build. Your game only shows them if its own program turns Vulkan on too.")
    end

    private action MakeItReal
        Subsection("Make it Real")
        Paragraph("It's complete. The perfect scene. It's cool. It's clever. It's got monsters. They're
amazing! Publish it! Hold on. Scenes are cool, but static. Just because there are images in the application
does not mean the game is ready to be published on an app store. The application needs movement, game logic
and much more. Every item placed in a scene can be found and changed later in code; the next page, "):Link("Manipulating a Running Scene", "/tutorials/Scenes/manipulate.html"):Text(", shows how.")
        Paragraph("The first step is to get the scene into a running program.")
    end

    private action LoadingProgram
        Subsection("A Program That Loads a Scene")
        Paragraph("To load a scene, use the following template:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/Runner.quorum")

        Paragraph("This template looks like a normal game template, as described in the games tutorials.
The first difference is "):Code("UseVulkan(true)"):Text(" in "):Code("Main"):Text(", which turns on Vulkan before the game starts. The second is one action, "):Code("LoadScene"):Text(". It reads a scene file, works out what is inside it, and adds each item, camera and
light to the game. The path is relative to the project's folder, so "):Code("Scenes/Scene.qs"):Text(" is the scene file in the project's Scenes folder. If the scene has a different name,
change the path to match.")

        Paragraph("A new scene project in Quorum Studio already has a program like this in "):Code("SourceCode/Main.quorum"):Text(". Quorum Studio's version makes a "):Code("File"):Text(" and gives it the path first, then calls "):Code("LoadScene"):Text(" with the file. Both do the same thing. Quorum Studio's version doesn't turn on Vulkan
yet, so add the "):Code("UseVulkan(true)"):Text(" line to "):Code("Main"):Text(" yourself. For this reason, running a new scene project straight away loads whatever
was placed in its scene and starts the game.")
    end

    private action RunningTheScene
        Subsection("Running the Scene")
        Paragraph("To run the scene, use any of these:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("the green Run button on the toolbar;")
        ListItem("the Run menu, then Run;")
        ListItem("the keys for Run.")
        Table("Keys for running a project", "media/scenes/Running/RunKeys.csv")
        Paragraph("Quorum Studio builds the project, then the game window opens with the scene
loaded. The camera in the game starts where the scene's camera was saved, so the view matches the scene
editor's.")

        Paragraph():Strong("A note on focus:"):Text(" in the scene editor itself, Cmd+R or Ctrl+R sets the selected item's scale back to 1.
If an item is selected and nothing runs, use the Run button or the Run menu instead.")
    end

    private action PhysicsAndGravity
        Subsection("3D Scenes: Physics and Gravity")
        Paragraph("A 3D scene can be set up in the editor so items fall or roll, but the template above
doesn't turn physics on. Quorum Studio's 3D scene template adds two lines after "):Code("LoadScene"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/SceneRunning/RunnerPhysics3D.quorum")

        Paragraph():Strong("Order matters."):Text(" A scene file remembers whether physics was on in each layer, and the editor saves it
as off. Loading the scene sets it again, so "):Code("EnablePhysics3D(true)"):Text(" goes after "):Code("LoadScene"):Text(". Gravity starts at zero, so without "):Code("SetGravity3D"):Text(" nothing falls.")

        Paragraph("Physics only moves the items that ask for it. In the Properties panel, an item that
should fall needs Physics Enabled checked and Responsiveness set to Responsive. Ground that should hold things
up needs Physics Enabled and Unmovable. "):Link("Properties", "/tutorials/Scenes/properties.html"):Text(" explains these, and "):Link("Gravity in 3D", "/tutorials/physics/gravity3D.html"):Text(" explains gravity in code.")
    end

    private action HearingWhatHappens
        Subsection("Hearing What Happens: A Beep on Every Bounce")
        Paragraph("Watching a ball fall only works if you can see the screen. A game can also tell
you what happened with sound, so anyone can follow along, including people who use a screen reader. The
program below plays a short high beep whenever two items start touching, such as the ball hitting the floor,
and a lower beep when they stop touching, such as the ball leaving the floor on a bounce:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/SceneRunning/RunnerBeep.quorum")

        Paragraph():Code("Main is Game, CollisionListener3D"):Text(" means this game also listens for collisions, and "):Code("AddCollisionListener(me)"):Text(" tells the 3D layer to send them to it. Each time two items with Physics Enabled start
touching, Quorum calls "):Code("BeginCollision"):Text(", and each time they stop, "):Code("FinishCollision"):Text(". "):Code("MakeBeep"):Text(" builds each sound from numbers instead of loading a sound file: a tenth of a second
of a smooth wave, 880 times a second for the high beep and 440 for the low one.")

        Paragraph("Listen to a bouncing ball and you'll hear high, low, high, low, with the gaps
between the beeps getting shorter as the bounces get smaller. No beeps at all means nothing touched. That
makes sound a useful way to test a physics scene.")
    end

    private action ScenesWithTerrain
        Subsection("Scenes with Terrain")
        Paragraph("A scene can have a terrain: the ground of a 3D world, with hills and valleys. Terrain
is drawn with Vulkan, and it is also solid ground for physics, so the program above, with "):Code("UseVulkan(true)"):Text(" in "):Code("Main"):Text(" and physics turned on after "):Code("LoadScene"):Text(", is exactly what a scene with terrain needs.")

        Paragraph("If you forget "):Code("UseVulkan(true)"):Text(", the program stops while loading the scene and says: "):DoubleQuote():Output("This scene has a
terrain, and a terrain needs Vulkan for now."):DoubleQuote():Text(" The fix is to add that line to "):Code("Main"):Text(" and run again.")

        Paragraph("Terrain has its own pages, starting with "):Link("Terrain in a Scene", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrain.html"):Text(". Loading terrain in a game, and asking it questions, is in "):Link("Terrain in a Game", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainGames.html"):Text(".")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("This activity is the second half of the experiment you started in "):Link("Properties", "/tutorials/Scenes/properties.html"):Text(". There, you and your partner wrote a "):Strong("hypothesis"):Text(" for each physics change: what you thought the ball or cube would do, and why. Now that
you can run a scene, it's time to "):Strong("test"):Text(" those hypotheses. The goal is to test each hypothesis fairly, one change at a time,
record what really happens, and decide whether the evidence supports it. Don't write new predictions: use the
list you already made. Get it out, and make a table with four columns: the change, your hypothesis, what
actually happened, and whether the result "):Strong("supports"):Text(" or "):Strong("doesn't support"):Text(" the hypothesis.")

        Paragraph("Set up the same scene as before: an Unmovable floor (a Cube scaled to 10, 0.2 and 10)
and a Responsive Sphere 3 meters above it, both with Physics Enabled. Use the beeping program from "):Quoted("Hearing What Happens"):Text(", so every test can be heard as well as seen: a high beep when the ball
lands, and a low beep when it leaves the floor. Run it once without changing anything, so you know what normal
looks and sounds like. Then test your hypotheses one at a time, changing "):Strong("only one thing"):Text(" for each test and putting it back before the next:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Responsiveness:"):Text(" Unmovable, then Nonresponsive, then Responsive.")
        ListItem():Strong("Restitution:"):Text(" 0, then 0.5, then 1, then 1.5, with the floor's at 0.")
        ListItem():Strong("Mass:"):Text(" 1, then 100.")
        ListItem():Strong("Friction:"):Text(" a cube on the floor tilted to 20 degrees, with friction 0, then
0.5, then 1.")
        ListItem():Strong("Gravity:"):Text(" "):Code("0, -1.6, 0"):Text(", then "):Code("0, -25, 0"):Text(", then "):Code("0, 10, 0"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Physics Enabled:"):Text(" turned off on the floor.")
        ListItem():Strong("A value that doesn't make sense:"):Text(" a negative mass or restitution.")

        Paragraph("To measure instead of guessing, use a "):Strong("stopwatch"):Text(", such as the one on a phone; most phones' stopwatches can be used with a screen reader
too. Base each measurement on timing. One partner starts the stopwatch the moment the game window opens and
stops it at the first high beep: that's how long the ball took to fall. For bounces, use the lap button at each
high beep, and compare the times between beeps: a higher bounce takes longer to come back down, so longer gaps
mean bouncier. For the friction test, time how long the cube takes from landing until its last beep, when it
slides off the end of the floor. Run each test three times and use the middle time, since starting and
stopping a stopwatch by hand is never exact. If you hear no beep at all, that's a result too: write down that
nothing touched.")

        Paragraph("Write every time and sound in your table. When you're done, count how many
hypotheses the tests supported. For each one they didn't support, explain what you now think is going on.
Finally, choose one result where the simulation behaves differently from the real world, and explain whether
that's a mistake in your hypothesis or a limit of the simulation.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("A scene is data: a file that says what is in the world and where. A program brings
it to life, and a graphics engine draws it, with Vulkan for the newest features. Testing your hypotheses one
change at a time is how scientists, and programmers, find out whether their ideas about a system are right.
Which of your results surprised you most, and what would you test next? So far the program only loads the
scene. What would a game need to know about the items in the scene to make one of them move when a key is
pressed?")
    end
end
